.SS noust fleet authorize
Let a central manage this server, and print the join code for it.
.TP
.B \-\-central\-key
The central's public key line for this server, as 'noust node key' printed it.
.TP
.B \-\-name
The central's name.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-user
Account the central's tunnel logs in as. The default is created and restricted in sshd; root needs \-\-i\-understand. Default: noust\-tunnel.
.TP
.B \-\-i\-understand
Confirm \-\-ssh\-user root: a key in root's authorized_keys can create Unix sockets as root, however it is restricted.
.TP
.B \-\-replace\-root\-key
A central's old public key to take out of root's authorized_keys once the new line is in place, whatever its comment (a 0600 copy of the file is kept). 'noust node migrate\-tunnel' prints it for a node a 3.0 central authorized as root.
.TP
.B \-\-access
The most any central may do on this server: read, deploy (operate and update applications) or admin. Unchanged when omitted (admin on a server that never set it).
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-host\-access, \-\-no\-host\-access
Let centrals reach the host itself: change how this server is reached (SSH keys, sshd, firewall, accounts) and make root\-equivalent changes (raw units, cron commands, backup hooks, raw site configuration), on top of \-\-access admin. Off unless set.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-self
Authorize even though this server looks like the central that issued the key (testing only).
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Replace an older token and move a background console to a service without asking.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result, join code included, as JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-reason
Why this change is made (a change reference, a ticket). Recorded with the command in the audit log; required by the ens\-medium security profile.

